#: ../../faq.rst
msgid "Q: I get an error in the IPython console running my code! Help!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:395
msgid "First, make sure the error you are seeing is not a bug in your code. To confirm this, try running it in any standard Python interpreter. If the error still occurs, the problem is likely with your code and a site like `Stack Overflow <Stack Overflow_>`__ might be the best place to start. Otherwise, start at the :ref:`troubleshooting-basic` section of our troubleshooting guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst
msgid "Q: Code completion/help doesn't work; what can I do?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:406
msgid "If nothing is displayed in the calltip, hover hint or :ref:`panes-help` pane, make sure the object you are inspecting has a docstring, and try executing your code in the :ref:`panes-console` to get help and completions there. If this doesn't work, try restarting the LSP server by clicking the :guilabel:`LSP: Python` item in the status bar at the bottom of Spyder's main window, and selecting the :guilabel:`Restart Python Language Server` option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:409
msgid "Spyder showing code completion status bar item with option to restart"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:412
msgid "For more information, go to the :ref:`troubleshooting-common-completion` section in the :ref:`troubleshooting-common` page of our troubleshooting guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst
msgid "Q: I get the message \"An error occurred while starting the kernel\". How do I fix this?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:418
msgid "First, make sure your version of Spyder-Kernels is compatible with that of Spyder. See the table in the :ref:`troubleshooting-common-kernel-version` section of the troubleshooting guide to check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:421
msgid "To install the correct version, copy the suggested command Spyder displays for you in the error message and run it in a system terminal (Anaconda Prompt on Windows if using Conda) with the Python environment you want to use activated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:423
msgid "Spyder showing IPython Console with error staring the kernel due to an unsupported Spyder-Kernels version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:426
msgid "For more information, go to the :ref:`troubleshooting-common-kernel` section in the :ref:`troubleshooting-common` page of our troubleshooting guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:434
msgid "About Spyder"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst
msgid "Q: What's Spyder's licensing situation? Is commercial use allowed?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:439
#, python-format
msgid "Spyder is 100% free and open source; there is no paid version or prohibition on commercial use. It is developed by its international user community, and supported by its users through `OpenCollective <OpenCollective_>`__ and by its generous sponsoring organizations, including `Quansight <Quansight_>`__ and `NumFOCUS <NumFOCUS_>`__. Our source code, standalone installers and most of our distribution methods (Pip/PyPI, Linux distros, MacPorts, WinPython, etc) can be freely redistributed, used and modified by anyone, for any purpose, including commercial use. For more details about the situation with Anaconda, see :ref:`anaconda-license`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst
msgid "Q: What does Anaconda's commercial licensing restrictions mean for Spyder?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:452
msgid "If you installed Spyder via any method other than the Anaconda or Miniconda distributions, or install it from the ``conda-forge`` channel with those two distributions, you are not affected by any restrictions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../faq.rst:454
#, python-format
msgid "If you use a copy of Spyder installed by default with the Anaconda distribution, or installed from the ``defaults``/``anaconda`` channel, Anaconda's `Terms of Service <Terms of Service_>`__ have restrictions on larger (>200 employee) for-profit enterprises on a large scale. However, these terms only apply to the package infrastructure (the full Anaconda distribution and the ``defaults`` conda channel). Instead, you can simply download the similar `Miniforge <Miniforge_>`__ distribution, which is 100% open source and nearly identical to Miniconda (and only different from Anaconda in that it does not bundle the Python packages installed by default in the Anaconda ``base`` environment, which we recommend you avoid using anyway given any problems in it can break your whole installation). Then, simply install the packages you need (including Spyder, if you aren't using our recommended :ref:`install-standalone`) with ``conda`` as you usually do. Miniforge will automatically use the community-maintained Conda-Forge repository, which has a much wider variety of packages and is generally more up to date than the Anaconda equivalent, in addition to being free of any commercial restrictions. For more, see our :ref:`install-guide`."
msgstr ""

